cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "cut-at-k: cut a stream at every point and check what the consumer is left with"
message: "If you use this harness or its figures, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Redouane
    alias: roshcompanylabs
    affiliation: ROSH Company Labs
    website: "https://github.com/roshcompanylabs"
repository-code: "https://github.com/roshcompanylabs/cut-at-k"
url: "https://github.com/roshcompanylabs/cut-at-k#readme"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - streaming
  - conformance testing
  - differential testing
  - server-sent events
  - truncation
  - large language models
abstract: >-
  A stream that stops early is not a rare case: a proxy times out, a producer
  dies, someone presses stop. cut-at-k replays an event stream through a client
  once whole and once for every prefix of it, and asks two questions of each
  cut. Did the bytes before the cut change — truncation may lose the tail, it
  must never alter the head. And does the client report the truncated run
  differently from the complete one. The second is the one that is quiet when it
  is wrong, which is why a loop finds it where reading the code does not.

  The worked example runs the harness over AG-UI's published conformance corpus
  through a real HttpAgent over HTTP/SSE. The recording is committed and names
  the client version that produced it, so every figure re-derives from one
  command. `probes/` asks the same question of other implementations, and
  records the ones that answer it correctly as well as the one that does not.
